- F-26 **Under walk, when one of two applicable declarations has static parameters of its own, the call chooses on their declared domains where exactly one lies inside the other, not by the order in which they are written.** It builds P-45, the choice among overloads by declared parameter types, under walk. A declaration has static parameters of its own if it is a generic function or constructor, or a functional method with static parameters in a trait or object without any. Its declared domain, walk's symbolic instance, is its parameter types with each type parameter standing for itself under its bounds. Where neither declared domain lies inside the other, where each lies inside the other, or where neither declaration has static parameters of its own, walk compares the domains of the instances that the call makes. So a functional method of a generic trait or object is compared on its instance, save two generic ones one inside the other, which stop with an error when an extending object's first instance is made (F-405), and a generic dotted method is chosen by F-253 (a generic method at a dotted call, also chosen on declared domains), except two whose bounds differ only in the api of a type of one simple name, of which the object's is kept (row 1134). If the chosen declaration is generic and its instance takes an argument only by a conversion, walk converts the argument and dispatches the converted arguments again, so the most specific declaration for the converted values runs. In two cases walk keeps the chosen instance, with no second dispatch: an overloaded dotted method, and a position whose conversion `Coercions.coercionFor` does not find, such as a tuple converted element by element. Two generic overloads whose static parameters agree in name, kind and bounds share one symbolic instance, even if their `where` clauses differ; no test or library declaration has such a pair (row 478). Gated by `tests/DispatchDeclaredDomain.fss` and `DispatchConvertedAgain.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-dispatch/REPORT.md` sections 5.1, 5.2 and 5.5.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C1. Since climb batch 36's rung G, two generic functional methods of a generic trait, one inside the other, stop at an extending object's first instance (row 780, rewritten), so the sentence on the comparison on the instance read wider than the tree. The Detail's glosses of its sections were cut to keep the size.
- F-76 **The compiled checker accepts the specification's `comprises` example of the Meet Rule (`Specification/advanced/overloading.tex`, "The Meet Rule for Functions") by a coverage check used only in overload checking, not in its subtype test, and types a call whose argument's static type lies below closed traits and above the types that they list by the intersection of the return types of its most specific declarations, when they tie without coercion.** It builds P-60, which reads `comprises` at the level of values: several declarations below two overloads serve as their meet if together they cover it. Open: coverage refuses a pair when a declaration that it compares has static parameters (row 546). It takes the intersection where the tied declarations, and every declaration more specific than one of them that the call may run, have no static parameters, the boundary of the text (`Specification/appendices/changes.tex`, `revival-meet`); F-408 gives how it decides, refuses or keeps any other tie. On the compiled path, a method call on a value typed by the intersection runs on the conjunct that has the checker's chosen declaration (F-386), and a generic declaration instantiated at an intersection type dies as it loads (row 559). The checker checks a `comprises` clause only at the closed trait's immediate subtypes, so a type in another component below a generic subtrait escapes it (row 487). Its verdict on an overload set can depend on the components compiled before it in the same JVM (row 547). Gated by `compiler_tests/*Comprises*` and `*CoverageReturn*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-return-type-rule/decision-record.md` sections 3 and 4.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C4. The clause left out the tied declarations' own condition, which rung K's tieType reads (item 3 of the section) and revival-meet states (Specification/appendices/changes.tex:3714-3717 at 61b50cd34). 'Decide the tie, or it is refused' left out the tie that the checker keeps by place among one unit's own declarations (row 1136), which F-408 owns.
- F-225 **In a self-typed trait with no `comprises` clause, or one that lists only `...`, the compiled checker types `self` in the method bodies as `C[\X\] & X`, the trait met with the implementing type, read from the trait's F-bound; the methods' signatures keep `self` at the plain trait type `C[\X\]`, save in the overloading check's exclusion test (F-277) and in the Return Type check of a dotted method redeclared below the trait (row 1094), and the checker refuses an extender at another type.** It builds P-146. A self-typed trait is a trait `C[\X\]` whose parameter `X` extends `C[\X\]`. The Return Type check reads `self` as `C[\X\] & X` in the trait's signature where an object's dotted method redeclares the trait's method at the same parameter types, so it refuses a narrower return type; a getter so redeclared reads the plain type and compiles (F-394). That reading of a dotted method departs from P-146 and P-157, which keep the plain signatures for the Return Type Rule (row 1094). The checker also takes a parameter bounded by a self-typed trait that the trait extends at that parameter, as the team's `Containment` mixins are: a default of the coordinator's. In a trait whose `comprises` clause lists types, `self` in the bodies is instead the trait met with the union of those types, and, as in a self-typed trait, the signatures keep `self` at the plain trait type and the exclusion test reads the meet. P-146 names the self-typed traits only; this reading of a `comprises` trait is a default of a batch's plan, which the curator has not decided. For example, `object Bad extends Integral[\ZZ32\]` is refused, since `Bad` is not a `ZZ32`. The rule accepts every self-typed body of the library but `Integral`'s `numerator`, which each integer type declares instead (P-146). Walk has no such rule and loads what it refuses. A compiled default body that returns `self` at the self-type parameter runs, also where the parameter is an object type, as in `object Pt(…) extends Shape[\Pt\]` (row 695, fixed). F-377 gives the cast that does it, also at a conditional's arm and at a closure's result (rows 1051 and 1050, fixed). A default body that calls a method on a value of the self-type parameter runs, also where the object lists the trait first (row 1052, fixed); F-385 gives how, and the generic object for which it may not hold. The rule refuses, rightly, a team declaration, `PureList`'s `List` (row 355). The compiler's prelude writes the self type instead as `comprises T` on `StandardTotalOrder` and `Equality` (`Library/CompilerAlgebra.fsi`), and that spelling does not bind the one library, since Naden's last text puts the self-type idiom in bounds only, not as a first-class type (`Papers/Types/journal/justificationOfRTR.tex:626-630`), and Steele's own message on `655e65d53` gives the self type as the trait met with the `comprises` clause, which this rule reads from the F-bound. Gated by `compiler_tests/SelfType*` and `XXXSelfType*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-self-type/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2, 4 and 7; `compile-ladder/rung-self-type-exclusion/REPORT.md` section 1.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C6. Fork K1 of climb batch 36, the planner's default, built the reading of a comprises trait past P-146's words (CLIMB-BATCH-36.md, "For the record-landing workflow"); review-queue item 621 holds his question and names F-225. The pointer to F-408 was cut, since F-225 owns the rule. Moved out to keep the size: the MIN and MAX example, which F-377 gives, and two line numbers of CompilerAlgebra.fsi (:16, :24).
- F-232 **The syntax tree's hashes do not depend on the JVM's identity hashes, except at inference variables, walk's wrapped values and the nodes that hold them.** Every generated node's hash starts from the hash of its class's name: makeAST's post-processing of ASTGen's output writes that start, and so do the in-repo generators (`build.xml:698-716`). A field whose type holds an `Option` is hashed by `NodeHash.of` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nodes_util/NodeHash.java`), since the PLT `Option` classes hash by identity. So hash maps and sets of nodes that hold neither exception iterate in one order whatever code asked for an identity hash before, and so do the apis that `StaticChecker.checkApis` takes in turn. The exceptions hash through identity hashes: an inference variable's id is a bare `Object` (`nodes_util/NodeFactory.java:1166-1184`), but no output of the count stage moves through its hash; walk's `_WrappedFValue` hashes its `FValue` by walk's own hash, which can be an identity hash (`interpreter/evaluator/_WrappedFValue.java:170-177`; row 430). `DeclaredFunction`s, entries of the checker's index and not nodes, still hash by identity, but since row 737's repair no choice of `ApiTypeExtractor` depends on their order (F-254), and no choice among a call's declarations does (F-408). Gated by `NodeHashJUTest`, and row 737's repair by `OmittedReturnTypeFromApi`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-ast-hashes/REPORT.md` sections 4.2 to 4.4.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C37. Since climb batch 36's rung K (fork K4) the checker takes a call's candidates in the order of their declarations' places, so their hashes order no choice among them (CLIMB-BATCH-36.md, "For the record-landing workflow").
- F-277 **The compiled checker's overloading exclusion test reads the `self` of a trait whose self type comes from its F-bound as `C[\X\] & X`, and each static parameter at the top of the two domains' meet as its declared bounds; every other rule of overloading keeps the plain signatures.** It builds P-157. It reads `self` in a trait whose `comprises` clause lists types as the trait met with the union of those types too, a batch's default that the curator has not decided (F-225). The second arrow is made from the declaration's own parameters (`FunctionalMethod.ast`) only where they differ from `parameters()` (`scala_src/typechecker/OverloadingChecker.scala`), and the bounds are read in one more conjunct of the reduction's constraint, alone and never beside the parameter (`scala_src/types/TypeSchemaAnalyzer.scala`, `meetEmptyWithBounds`, a third way to be valid in `OverloadingOracle.excludes`). With the full check on, the 8 pairs between `AdditiveGroup`'s `+` and `-` and the generic scalar block clear (191 to 183, no library line), and the count stage under it is 98. `tint` stays refused, `Box[\Bottom\]` being an instance of both domains (row 786); row 713's pair is accepted and runs, and walk refuses it at load; walk loads the vector pairs' shape in a program's own component too, reading `self` so in its exclusion test (F-318). Gated by `compiler_tests/Exclusion*`, `XXXExclusion*` and `OverloadTwoBoundsClosedTraitExclusion`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-self-type-exclusion/REPORT.md` sections 1, 2 and 4.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C38. Since climb batch 36's rung K the exclusion test reads the own parameters through selfTyped, which finds them different from parameters() in a comprises trait too (item 1 of the section). The meet is with the union of the listed types (F-225), and the reading is fork K1's default, which review-queue item 621 holds.
- F-334 **The compiled checker orders a call's candidates, a coercion's candidates and a trait's ancestors by a linear extension of their partial order, read once a pair, so no call stops it at 32 candidates or more.** `scala_src/useful/PartialOrder.scala` gives `minimal` and `linear`, used at `Functionals.scala:972`, `:1101-1121`, `CoercionOracle.scala:363-382` and `Formula.scala:610`; a call's candidates come in the order of their declarations' places, and F-408 gives how a tie among the most specific is decided, refused or kept by place. On the one library the checker's results are those of the sort it replaced, and the distance checks `value object Char` to its end (`#crash none`). Tests: `compiler_tests/PartialOrderCandidatesCompiled`, `compiler_tests/TieAmongMostSpecificCompiled`, `PartialOrderJUTest`, the distance stage. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-partial-order/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2, 6 and 8.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C10. The sentence left out the tie that the checker keeps by place, with no error, among one unit's own declarations (row 1136), which F-408 gives.
- F-342 **The compiled checker converts a numeral where nothing fixes a type into its default, refuses one out of range, and refuses a numeral type written other than as a parameter's type.** Where no place's type has a coercion from the numeral's type (an untyped binding, `Any`, `Object`, `Number`, an unbounded type parameter, a receiver, a join, a result with no declared return type), a numeral becomes a `ZZ32`, or a `ZZ64` or `ZZ` by its value, or an `RR64` with a radix point. A type parameter that numerals alone fix takes the narrowest default holding them all, and the conversion into the default ranks as no coercion. A numeral outside a bounded number type's range, or rounding to an infinity, is a static error, also as a tuple's element at a tuple type and in a result position of an `if`, a `case`, a `do` block or a `try`; a program's type with no coercion from the numeral's type refuses a numeral. `NaturalNumeral` and `RadixPointNumeral` are written in a program only as a parameter's type, never as a type that an object or a trait extends. A `nat` or `int` parameter read as a value is a `ZZ32`; one of a numeral coercion's four numbers is a `ZZ` where nothing fixes a type and converts where a place fixes a bounded number type (`scala_src/typechecker/staticenv/KindEnv.scala`, `getType`; `CoercionOracle.buildCoercion`; F-407). It builds P-173's checker, P-174's written-only rule, P-174's four numbers save at a call and across components (F-407) and P-40's size value; a `typecase`'s selector keeps its own numeral type (row 963). Tests: `compiler_tests/LiteralDefaultCompiled`, `LiteralDefaultResultCompiled`, `XXXLiteralTypeUnwritable.test`, `XXXNumeralBeyondZZ32Range.test`, `XXXNumeralBeyondRangeInTuple.test`, `XXXNumeralJoinBeyondRange.test`, `XXXNumeralTypeExtendedRefused.test`, `NatValueZZ32Compiled`, `NumeralStaticDesignCompiled` (a `v` beyond `ZZ64`). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-checker/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-numeral-coercion-body/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C12. The claim that the checker builds P-174's four numbers left out the compiled path's two departures, a number at a call (row 1132) and a number in another component's coercion that is not refused statically (row 1131), which F-407 names.
- F-376 **A compiled dispatcher of a generic declaration casts an arm's result to its own range.** Where an overload set's principal declaration returns one of its static parameters, the dispatcher that `OverloadSet` writes (`generateCall`, `generateLeafCall`) casts the result of each arm with another declared return type to that parameter, which the class loader instantiates; for an arm instantiated at run time the cast takes the place of the cast to the arm's declared return type. So a generic declaration beside a declaration on an object type verifies at every instance, where without the cast the class of the generic's instance at another object type fails, 'VerifyError: Bad return type'. A dispatcher whose range is no static parameter has no such cast (row 699). Test: the `run` step of `compiler_tests/OverloadReturnObjectDomain`; its call at `Box[\Square\]` fits two declarations, `keep[\T\]` and `Box`'s `keep(self)`, of which the second is the more specific by its declared parameter type and so the checker's choice by F-408's rules, read, not observed in a run. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-return-rule-object/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C39. Since climb batch 36's rung K the checker decides a tie by the declared signatures, in no order that a build varies (row 935 closed; row 499 noted, its dispatcher half open); the two declarations read in compiler_tests/OverloadReturnObjectDomain.fss:10 and :16.
- F-396 **The one library compiles to jars over a second set of run-time classes, and a program compiled against them can run on the compiled path, as one that prints a line, calls a varargs declaration, reduces over a range or compares two strings does.** Under `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one` the onelibrary track compiles the one library's fourteen components first, in one `.test` (`OneLibraryJUTest.java:44`), and a program compiled against them runs with the JVM's verifier on, as `Hello` does. The run time's classes that name a library's types come in two sets under one set of names: `ProjectFortress/build` for the prelude, and `ProjectFortress/build-one`, built from `ProjectFortress/src-one` by `ant compileAll`, for the one library, which the class path puts first under the selection. The one library's seven number types, `Boolean`, `Char` and `FlatString` are objects whose classes the code generator writes over payload classes (`NamingCzar.java`), all value objects but `FlatString`, a reference object (`Library/FlatString.fss:32`), and a helper's raise names its exception by its simple name, which each set's `LibraryNames` maps to its library's class. Against the one library the compiled path still departs from walk, each at a row: `println` of a number or a boolean, which the export check's rule decides as much as the code generator (row 1098); `u.asString` in a generic body at `U extends ZZ32` (row 1101); the report of an uncaught exception (row 1102); a caught exception that a helper raises, which is not its object (row 1103); a reduction over a zip of two lists, or `=` of two lists (row 1137); a `Vector`'s `+` and `-` (row 643) and its `copy` (row 1138); a failing `assert` with a message, which dies with an `AbstractMethodError` in place of its `FAIL` line (row 1140). The track compiles the fourteen first since its programs use them. A call on `self` in `QQ comprises { Ratio }` takes `Ratio`'s declaration in every JVM (F-408). Tests: `one_library_tests/OneLibraryComponents.test`, `OneLibraryRunHello.test`, `OneLibraryCatchHelperRaises.test`, `OneLibraryRunStringEquality.test`, `OneLibraryVarargsArguments.test`, `OneLibraryRangeReduction.test`, `OneLibraryArr.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-first-run-one-library/REPORT.md`, "The design as built (a point to report)"; `compile-ladder/rung-one-library-run-walls/REPORT.md`.
  from: reviews/batch-36-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C18, C28. Finding 3 of the climb batch 36 review: the gate's step 7 ran tests/NumeralCoercionNumberAtCallWalk.fss under the one library, and its failing assert died, 'AbstractMethodError: Receiver class NativeArray$\=PrimImmutableArray... does not define or inherit ... map', from BIG || in assert (Library/FortressLibrary.fss:318); row 1140 is filed by this landing. To keep the size: the mechanisms of rows 1137, 643 and 1138 are their rows', a line number of NamingCzar.java was moved out, and the sentence on the checker's choice was cut to its consequence.
- F-408 **The compiled checker chooses among a call's declarations by their signatures as declared, takes them in the order of their places, and refuses a tie that the rules leave, save one among a unit's own declarations (row 1136).** It builds P-45, the choice by declared parameter types, and P-60's tie, with `self` in the signatures as F-225 gives. The candidates are in the order of their declarations' places, file then line then column; that order is a default of a batch's plan, which the curator has not decided. Of a tie's candidates, a declaration met twice counts once, and the one more specific than every other on its declared arrow decides. P-60's tie is typed by its intersection where the tied declarations, and every declaration more specific than one of them that the call may run, have no static parameters. Any other tie is 'Ambiguous call ...', save a tie that the overloading check reports instead: among the declarations of the unit, the component being checked, or among duplicates. Of a tie that no rule decides among the unit's own declarations, in a set that the overloading check accepts, the first by place is kept with no error (row 1136). The code generator casts `self` where the chosen declaration takes a listed type (F-377). So a call's choice and type no longer follow the JVM's identity hashes, and the one library's calls on `self` in `QQ` take `Ratio`'s declarations. Tests: `compiler_tests/GenericTiesDeclaredCompiled`, `ComprisesSelfListedTypeCompiled`, `ComprisesFunctionalMethodNarrowedReturn`, `CoverageReturnGenericFamily`, `OverloadGenericObjectTwoParams`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C23, C32. The order by place is fork K4 of climb batch 36, the planner's default; the curator's words of 07:0x UTC on 2026-11-24 ("File order cannot decide! There must be a language rule that decides definitively or its static error!", BOOT.md:11 at 99035e231) make it his question, which review-queue item 419 holds. The entry named no decision it builds (P-45, P-60). Its first sentence restated fork K1's signature rule, which F-225 owns. P-60's boundary gains the tied declarations' own condition, which tieType reads (item 3 of the section).
- F-409 **A class that the code generator writes holds each method once by name and descriptor: a bridge, a forwarder to an inherited body and a dispatch method among inherited declarations are written only where the class's own methods leave the name and descriptor free, and one bridge that two parents give is written once.** The class writer keeps the record and holds those methods to the class's end (`compiler/codegen/CodeGenClassWriter.java`). It follows `Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Method Declarations": a declaration of the type itself replaces an inherited one at the same parameter types. The one library's 2208 classes held 25 methods twice on the base and hold none; two methods whose descriptors become equal only at a generic class's instance are not caught. Tests: `one_library_tests/OneLibraryVarargsArguments.test`, `OneLibraryRangeReduction.test`, `OneLibraryArr.test`, `OneLibraryDot.test`, `compiler_tests/TraitBridgesWrittenOnce.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-one-library-run-walls/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `SKEPTIC.md` beside it.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-one-library-run-walls/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C24, C33. The claim, its mechanism and the 25 methods held twice on the base hold against rung C's report; forks C1 to C3 of climb batch 36 are not consequential. To keep the size, the method names in CodeGen.java (startBridges, dumpMethodChaining, overrideBridges, forObjectDeclPrePass, forTraitDecl) are the report's detail.
- F-139 **`RR32` is a sibling of `RR64` under `Number`, and `RR64` converts from it.** Nothing converts into `RR32`. Under `walk` two `RR32`s answer an `RR32`, and an `RR32` with an `RR64`, a float numeral or a `ZZ32` answers an `RR64`. A typed binding converts an `RR32`, but a declared return does not (row 387). On the compiled path `=` at the prelude's `RR32` overflows the stack, save `c = 0`, refused statically (row 528), and two `RR32`s answer an `RR64` (row 529). Gated by `tests/RR32SiblingRungV.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-rr32-sibling/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 11.
  from: reviews/batch-36-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C36. Since climb batch 36's rung K, c = 0 for c: RR32 is refused statically under the prelude, 'Ambiguous coercion in call to operator =' (row 528, rewritten); = of two RR32 values and a = 1.5 still overflow.
- F-257 **Under walk, a pair of declarations that the load check refuses after it reads the pair on its declared domains is refused in the name of the rule that the pair breaks.** The load check reads a pair on its declared domains where its parameter-by-parameter check refuses the pair and both declarations have static parameters of their own, or one has them and the other is a plain declaration or a generic trait's functional method that declares none of its own (F-252, F-27, F-318), and where that check finds a plain declaration inside an own generic's declared domain excluding at object types alone (F-375); it reads two generic dotted methods' symbolic instances one of whose declared domains lies inside the other's (`methodPairValid`). It reads a pair that the check orders only for the No Duplicates Rule (F-274). The messages open "fails the Return Type Rule:" and "fails the Meet Rule:", and the No Duplicates Rule's is walk's "fails because their parameter lists have the same types"; the report gives them whole. The first two contain the words "not found" because walk's reading is narrower than the rules: rows 539 and 779 hold valid pairs that walk refuses. A pair that is not read on its declared domains keeps the parameter check's message, such as "with generic type, at least one pair of parameters must have excluding types". Such a pair has a varargs parameter, parameter lists of different lengths, or an error while its instances are read; or it is of none of the kinds above, as two functional methods of generic traits (row 780), or it is the pair of instances that an explicit instantiation such as `f[\A, B\]` makes of two generic declarations of one name (`XXXGenericOverload2`). The message does not decide which pairs load. Gated by `tests/GenericReturnRuleInvariantWalk`, `GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk`, `GenericBesidePlainReturnRuleWalk` and `GenericPairTupleReturnWalk` (Return Type), `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`, `GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainNoMeetWalk` (Meet), and `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk` and `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` (No Duplicates). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 3.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C9, C30. Rows 780 and 1133, valid sets that walk now refuses at load, keep the parameter check's message, 'at least one pair of parameters must have excluding types', and read under the sentence on the pairs that are not read on their declared domains; row 780 is named there as its example. The Return Type tests' list had two "and"s; two sentences were joined to keep the size.
- F-341 **Walk's numeral is its default, carrying the numeral's four numbers, and converts from them where its place fixes a type.** An integer numeral is a `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ` by its value, a radix-point numeral the `RR64` nearest its value, one value per site (`interpreter/evaluator/values/Numerals.java`). At a typed binding, an assignment, a field, a parameter, a declared return type and a coercion it converts through the place type's coercion from `NaturalNumeral` or `RadixPointNumeral`, run with the four numbers bound as numbers that convert where they stand, a `ZZ` where nothing fixes a type (F-407), one result per site and type; a folded constant at a library number type converts from its exact value, outside the coercion; a numeral out of the type's range, one that rounds to an infinity at `RR32` or `RR64`, one at a type with no coercion from it, or one outside its coercion's `where` clause is refused there when it runs; a store keeps its default alone. Dispatch fits it at its default, unless its own coercion reaches a declaration strictly below, which then runs (F-366), else by its own type's coercions only, and reads a tie at the default; a type parameter that several numerals fix takes the narrowest default holding them all. So `q: QQ = 0.1` is 1/10 and `y: RR64 = 0.5` is `SEQV` a computed 0.5, and `h = 0.5` is `true` for `h: QQ = 1/2`, by `QQ`'s `=`, which the numeral reaches by its own coercion, not the library's `=` over `Any` (F-366; `tests/NumeralEqualsNumberWalk`). At load walk refuses a numeral type written as a type that a trait or an object extends, as a type that a trait's `comprises` clause lists, as a static argument, or in a return type, a variable's, a field's or an object's value parameter's type; one written as a static parameter's bound or a `typecase` arm's type loads (row 1135). Tests: `tests/NumeralTypesWalk.fss`, `NumeralOwnTypeWalk.fss`, `NumeralNoCoercionRefusedWalk.fss`, `NumeralAtReturnWalk.fss`, `NumeralWhereRefusedWalk.fss`, `NumeralFoldedConstantLibraryWalk.fss`, `NumeralRoundsToInfinityWalk.fss`, `NumeralPointRoundsToInfinityWalk.fss`, `NumeralPointRoundsToInfinityAtParameterWalk.fss`, `NumeralFoldedRoundsToInfinityWalk.fss`, and the load check's `NumeralType*Walk.fss`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-types/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-leftovers/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-load-check-generic-numeral/REPORT.md`, "Row 981: a numeral type in a comprises clause (fork L3)"; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C11, C26. "An object's value parameters among the fields" read two ways; rung G's load check reads an object's value parameters (forObjectDecl) as it reads a field's type. To keep the size, the eight load-check tests are named by their pattern, which also holds NumeralTypeWrittenWalk.fss, a load-check test that the list left out.
- F-347 **Every numeral's place on the compiled path whose type names no type parameter is one value made before the program runs: a JVM constant where the fold of its type's own numeral coercion ends in an `int`, `long`, `float` or `double`, else a static final filled once by the coercion's method of the four numbers as `ZZ` values.** The code generator folds the body of the place's type's coercion, the library's or a program's, over a fragment of constants, bound names, natives over the built-in types' representations and calls of functions and methods in the fragment (`compiler/codegen/NumeralCoercionFold.java`); the method, `coerce_T№natural` or `coerce_T№point`, is compiled once in the declaring component and called from any component, and a program type's minus on a negative constant runs once with it (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java`). A number of the four that the checker typed at a bounded number type converts in the method from the `ZZ` that holds it, and in the fold into the type's representation (F-407). A place whose type names a type parameter calls the method at each evaluation (row 957). A coercion whose body puts two calls of the program's own functions in one operator or one argument list, as `small(n) - small(m)` does, spawns a task, which runs in place inside the root task that initializes the component's class (F-348). Under walk a folded constant at a library type converts from its exact value. Tests: `compiler/desugarer/NumeralCoercionFoldJUTest`, `library_tests/NumeralFoldIntegers`, `NumeralFoldFloats`, `compiler_tests/NumeralAcrossComponent`, `NumeralMinusOnceCompiled`, `NumeralCoercionOnceCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-static-numerals/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-numeral-coercion-body/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C14, C29. The title and the new sentence hold against rung N's report: the method takes the four numbers as ZZ values and converts inside. The sentence's source names (CodeGen.convertNumber, compiler/runtimeValues/FZZ.java) are the report's detail, which F-407 points to.
- F-366 **On both paths a numeral at a call leaves the declaration that fits it at its default only for one that its own coercion reaches and whose declared parameter types lie strictly below, by subtyping.** It builds P-46's rule for a numeral at a call. P-46 does not word the order: the worker that built the rule chose subtyping, and the curator has read that choice as landed and let it stand, without wording a decision of his own. So `u = 0` and `0 = u` are `true`, and `u =/= 0` is `false`, for a zero `u` of `NN32`, `NN64` or `RR32`, but on the compiled path until the switch-over the checker refuses `u =/= 0` at `RR32` and `RR64` (row 1059) and `u = 0` at `RR32`, an ambiguous coercion (row 528); `h = 0.5` is `true` for `h: QQ = 1/2`; `k(5)` runs `k(x: NN32)` beside `k(x: Any)`; a `case` clause `0` matches a zero `NN32`; and a program's own number type with a numeral coercion compares with a numeral by its own `=`. `Equality[\T\]` declares `opr =/=(self, other:T)` as the negation of the type's `=` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:102`), which a type that extends `Equality` reaches before the `=/=` over `Any` (`:98`). The order between the declaration that the default fits and one that the coercion reaches is subtyping on the declared parameter types, not the coercion chapter's relation: `f(1.5)` beside `f(x: RR32)` and `f(x: RR64)` runs the `RR64` declaration, and `x^2` for an `RR64` keeps its integer power. A value's coercion is not ranked so. Where a call reaches two declarations only by coercion and they are not ordered, the one whose parameter the numeral's default fits as it is runs, whether they differ at the numeral alone or elsewhere too, so `1.0 s` for `s: ZZ32` checks against the one library at `RR64`'s juxtaposition. So a model line may compare a number of any type with a numeral, and write a float numeral beside an integer value, on both paths. The checker compares only where the declaration that fits has a numeral at a parameter that is not of the numeral's default type, so a call such as `i + 1` costs nothing more; the report says which choices walk keeps in its dispatch cache. Two departures: under walk a numeral beside a value typed `Any` is converted by the value's run-time type (row 1016), and on both paths a numeral at a bare type parameter's declaration does not go to a more specific one (row 1017). Source: `numeralAttempt` and `numeralTie` in `scala_src/typechecker/impls/Functionals.scala:980-1021`, `:1066`, `:1143`; `bestMatchForNumerals` and `belowByNumerals` in `interpreter/evaluator/values/OverloadedFunction.java:2422-2525`. Tests: `compiler_tests/NumeralMostSpecificCompiled`, `compiler_tests/NumeralEqualsNumberCompiled`, `compiler_tests/NumeralTieDefaultCompiled`, `tests/NumeralMostSpecificWalk`, `tests/NumeralEqualsNumberWalk`, `tests/NotEqualNumeral`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-most-specific/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why" and "5. Points to report".
  from: reviews/batch-36-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C34. Since climb batch 36's rung K (fork K8), u = 0 for u: RR32 is refused under the prelude, 'Ambiguous coercion in call to operator =', since Equality's = and RR64's are unordered (row 528, rewritten); u = u of two RR32 values still overflows the stack.
- F-377 **The compiled code casts `self` where the checker reads it as a listed type or as the self-type parameter.** `CodeGen.castToTypeBelow` casts a value that has self's type (the checker's `TraitSelfType` with a non-empty list: a trait whose `comprises` clause lists types, or a trait with a self-type parameter) to a declared trait type or type variable strictly below its trait. It casts at the argument of a call with one argument (a call with several arguments casts each already), also where the chosen declaration takes `self` at a listed type, as `QQ`'s calls on `self` that take `Ratio`'s (F-408), at a typed binding and an assignment, at a method's returned value, at each arm of a conditional or a `case` expression, at the conditional's type, and at a function expression's and a local function's result, also where the value is a typecase whose arms each have self's type. So `den(self)` for `den(r: R)` in `trait Q comprises { R }`, `twin(self): S = self` and `mx(self, other: T): T = if lt(other, self) then self else other end` in a self-typed trait verify and run (rows 991, 695, 1050 and 1051, fixed), as the one library's `QQ comprises { Ratio }` and `StandardTotalOrder`'s `MIN` and `MAX` need. A conditional or typecase whose arms are pairs that hold `self`, `MINMAX`'s shape, verifies and runs with no cast, since the declared tuple type is a JVM interface and the caller converts the result; a typecase whose arms are all `self` has a one-element union, which is cast. Only `self` gets the reading: the checker refuses a variable declared at the trait where a listed type is expected. A typecase with `self` in one arm and another value in the other gets no cast (row 1070), and a trait that lists a type at a `where`-clause variable gets none (row 307). An assignment from outside an object to its `var` field passes the value at the field's type, `self` at a listed type cast to it (F-406). No cast is added where the value's type is a subtype of the declared type by `extends`. Two casts are added where the verifier, which checks no interface type, needs none: at a declared trait type strictly below self's trait, a planner's default; and at an arm that is `self` where the conditional stands at a trait type, as `atQ(c: Boolean): Q = if c then self else R(0, 0) end` gains `checkcast R`, a worker's choice beyond the plan. The curator has decided neither. Tests: `compiler_tests/ComprisesValueAtListedTypeCompiled`, `ComprisesValueAtConditionalArmRun`, `ComprisesValueFromClosureRun`, `SelfTypeReturnSelfRun`, `SelfTypeReturnSelfAtTraitRun`, `SelfTypeArmRun`, `SelfTypeFromClosureRun`, `VarFieldAssignedSubtypeRun`, `ComprisesSelfListedTypeCompiled`, `ComprisesSelfAbstractFunctionalMethodCall`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-comprises-cast/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-self-arm-closure/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why"; the pairs and the all-`self` typecase: `reviews/model-comparison/probe-2026-11-18.md`, "Point 1" and "Point 2"; the call at a listed type's declaration: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C16, C27. Rung K's clause, its two tests and its Detail hold against item 4 of the section; the clause was reworded shorter, and a test name that the Tests list holds (VarFieldAssignedSubtypeRun) was cut from a sentence.
- F-405 **Walk runs the generic dotted methods of one name that a trait or an object declares together, each as its instance at symbolic static arguments.** An object takes each generic method of a name, alone or beside others of the name, with one symbolic instantiation for each name and, per static parameter, kind and bounds, with the method's own static parameters in the bounds compared by position, not by name (`interpreter/evaluator/values/Constructor.java`, `genericKey`): so an object's redeclaration of a trait's generic method at the same bounds under other names hides it, and two that differ in their bounds are overloads, except bounds that differ only in the api of a type of one simple name (row 1134). At load walk reads two such instances on their declared domains where one lies inside the other; a set of them whose overlap only a third covers is refused (row 1133), and so is, when an extending object's first instance is made, a nested pair of a generic trait's generic functional methods (row 780). An object that inherits two such methods of one name from two traits, neither more specific, with no declaration at their meet, loads and runs, where the Meet Rule refuses the set (row 1139). A call that writes no static argument infers them and dispatches among the instances, the inner declared domain first; a call that writes static arguments instantiates each generic method whose static parameters, by their number and kinds, take them, and chooses among those instances by the call's arguments, as a call of a top-level generic function does. A call on a receiver assumed at the trait stops (row 1126). Where a plain method shares the name with a generic one, a call that writes static arguments stops (row 744); of two generic functional methods of one name and one number of parameters, the second is never chosen (row 1123). Tests: `tests/GenericMethodOverloadArity.fss`, `GenericMethodOverloadSetWalk.fss`, `GenericOverloadStaticArgKindsWalk.fss`, `GenericMethodBoundsDifferWalk.fss`, whose object `Both` asserts walk's answers for row 1139's set, the departure. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-load-check-generic-numeral/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "Programs run old against new"; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-36-review.md, "Findings for the record, with no decision of his changed"; from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C20, C25. Finding 2 of the climb batch 36 review: the object Both of tests/GenericMethodBoundsDifferWalk.fss:30-38 runs under walk, and the checker refuses it under both libraries, 'Invalid overloading of tb in object Both' (the gate's step 7), as the Meet Rule for dotted methods does (Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:411-430); P-45 has walk and the checker accept the same pairs. Row 1139 is filed by this landing. To keep the size, the key's mechanism (bounds renamed by position, names without their apis, Indexed's map hiding Generator's) and methodPairValid's name are rung G's report's detail, and the call's sources (DottedMethodApplication.genericOverloadsAt, OverloadedFunction.compatible) rung L's, rung-walk-load-check-generic-numeral/REPORT.md, "What changed and why".
- F-407 **A numeral coercion's four numbers convert where they stand, on both paths, a `ZZ` where nothing fixes a type.** It builds P-174's rule for a coercion's body, with P-173's conversion. Walk binds each as a static number whose default is a `ZZ` (`interpreter/evaluator/values/Numerals.java`), which converts where its place fixes a type as a folded constant does and is refused there when the place runs. The checker types it `ZZ`, keeps it so at a call that a declaration takes so, converts it where its place fixes a bounded number type, also in a result position of a join there, and refuses at the numeral's place a number that does not fit, for a coercion of the numeral's own component. The code generator converts it from the `ZZ`. So `ZZ32`'s coercion is `= v` and a byte type's `Byte(v)`. Two departures, both on the compiled path: a number that does not fit, in another component's coercion, is not refused statically (row 1131); a number at a call runs the declaration that takes a `ZZ` (row 1132). Tests: `tests/NumeralCoercionBodyWalk.fss`, `NumeralCoercionNumberRangeWalk.fss`, `NumeralCoercionNumberAtCallWalk.fss`, `compiler_tests/NumeralCoercionBodyCompiled`, `XXXNumeralCoercionNumberRangeCompiled.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-coercion-body/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-numeral-coercion-body/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C22, C31. The entry named no decision; it builds P-174's sentence on a coercion's body (his words recorded 14:33Z on 2026-11-23, POSITIONS-history.md, P-174) with P-173's conversion; forks N1 and N2 of climb batch 36 are not consequential. To keep the size, the source names (Numerals.number and convert, Functionals.typedApplication and joinInto, CoercionOracle.buildCoercion, NumeralConversion.numberAt and numberRefusals, CodeGen.convertNumber, FZZ.numberAs*) and row 1131's mechanism (IntegerOverflow at class initialization, an infinity at RR64) moved to rung N's report and the row.
- F-174 **The specification states the type group's 2011 overloading model: declarations of one name may differ in their static parameters, and a declaration with static parameters is read as one declaration over all its instances within its bounds.** It states P-45 and P-42. The section "Declarations with Static Parameters" states the OOPSLA 2011 paper's three rules: No Duplicates, Return Type and Meet. It gives one more restriction as the compiled path's, not the language's. Where one generic declaration is more specific than another with as many static parameters, the two must declare them of the same kinds, position by position. Also, the more specific one's return type must be a subtype of the other's when their static parameters are matched by position, so the checker refuses an override whose return type reorders its static parameters (row 398, fixed). The section "Principles of Overloading" forbids overloading a functional whose single parameter's type is a type parameter with the bound `Any` written, as `T extends Any`. A type parameter with no `extends` clause, whose bound is also `Any`, is exempt. Both paths refuse such an overload: the checker in `OverloadingChecker.checkBoundAny`, and walk since row 534's fix. Neither path runs every set that the rules allow. Appendix I's entry "Overloading with static parameters" names the sets that each path fails. In row 1048, the checker refuses a generic declaration beside a plain one over a tuple of object types, as `ident[\T\](x: T): T` beside `ident(x: (Circle, Circle)): (Circle, Circle)`. In row 537, the code generator fails on two generic declarations whose bounds differ. In row 539, walk refuses at load the 2019 paper's valid set over an array list and a list. In rows 779 and 780, walk fails on two generic functional methods one of whose declared domains lies inside the other's: in a trait or object without static parameters it refuses them at load, and in a trait with static parameters it stops with an error when an extending object's first instance is made. Detail: `Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:134-150` and `:531-674`; `compile-ladder/rung-spec-overloading/decision-record.md` sections 3 and 4.
  from: reviews/batch-36-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C35. Since climb batch 36's rung G, walk stops on such a pair in a trait with static parameters at the extending object's first instance, before any call (row 780, rewritten).
- P-45 **Overloaded declarations may differ in their static parameters.** The specification drops its sentence that forbids this, and keeps it as superseded text. It states instead the overloading model of the type group's 2011 paper (`Papers/Types/`), which the compiled checker already used. The checker keeps the paper's three rules: No Duplicates, Meet and Return Type. It checks the Return Type Rule over every instance of a generic declaration that the argument types allow, not over one guessed instance. One rule of the old sentence survives: if one generic declaration is more specific than another, their static parameters must line up position by position, as Java's rule for overriding a generic method requires. The compiled checker tests it only where the two declarations have as many static parameters of their own (F-75). Walk chooses among overloads by the parameter types that each declaration declares, not by the order in which they are written. At load, walk judges each pair of two generic declarations by the checker's rules on their declared parameter types. It does not refuse a pair only because none of their parameter types exclude, as when one declaration is more specific than the other. Walk and the checker must accept the same pairs. The library repairs, by declarations of its own, the overload sets that the checker refused, and no line of microGPT's two programs changes. Where walk or the checker still judges a pair against these rules: walk compares two functional methods of a generic trait or object on the instance that a call makes (F-26); both paths refuse the paper's own valid example (row 539); walk refuses two generic functional methods of a trait without static parameters whose declared domains lie one inside the other, and stops at an extending object's first instance on such a pair in a generic trait (rows 779 and 780); and walk and the checker disagree on three kinds of pair (rows 786 to 788).
  from: reviews/batch-36-review.md, "Corrections owed in the record"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C43. Since climb batch 36's rung G, walk refuses row 780's valid pair at the extending object's first instance, before any call (row 780, rewritten). Brought in line with the tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31. His decision, where it applies and what it requires are unchanged.
- P-60 **The specification reads a `comprises` clause at the level of values.** `trait S comprises { U, V }` says that every value of `S` is a value of `U` or of `V`. So another trait may stand between `S` and its listed types. The specification defines "covers" once: a type covers another when every value of the second is a value of the first. The Meet Rule gains a case for closed traits. Where the rule asks for a declaration at the meet of two overloads' parameter types, several declarations below both serve, if together they cover the meet. So a call whose argument's static type stands between closed traits and their listed types may have several most specific declarations. The call's type is the intersection of their return types, and the declaration that runs is unique only at run time. The compiled checker uses coverage only in its overloading rules, to accept an overload set and to justify a call's tie among several most specific declarations, and not in its general subtype test. Why: the proof addendum (`reviews/comprises-type-level-proof-addendum.md`) shows this sound for ground declarations, at calls that need no coercion. A ground declaration's types hold no type variable, and `G[\ZZ32\]` is ground. The addendum shows it for coverage used only in that way, and leaves any wider use to a review of its own. A call that needs a coercion is outside the proof too (F-76 gives what the compiled checker does there). A declaration with static parameters, whose instance dispatch must choose at run time, is outside the proof: one instance would have to meet every one of the return types at once. The typing by the intersection is not extended to it, and the specification states that boundary. Row 551: walk checks no `comprises` clause declared outside the main component.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-checker-most-specific/REPORT.md, "Sentences of the specification"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C42. Rung K of climb batch 36 closed row 543 (da5f70777): the checker gives the intersection within the boundary that the text states (revival-meet), which the sentence before already holds. Brought in line with the tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31; review.1 of climb batch 36's merged-diff review. His decision, where it applies and what it requires are unchanged.
- P-174 **A numeral's type exists only at compile time: the Working Draft's `NaturalNumeral` or, with a radix point, `RadixPointNumeral`, below `Numeral`, with its four numbers as static parameters; every number type, the library's too, takes a numeral through a coercion generic over the four numbers.** The four numbers are the digit count `n`, the digits after the radix point `m`, the radix `r` and the digits read as one integer in that radix `v` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/expressions/literals.tex:83-129`), so `3.125` is a `RadixPointNumeral[\4, 3, 10, 3125\]`; the four are naturals of any size; this implementation's `NN32` bound (P-40) is for sizes. A coercion's body is a computation at compile time, with no method invocation: a number used as a value there converts where it stands, as a numeral does (P-173), into the type that its place fixes, and is refused statically where it does not fit, so `value object Byte(b: ZZ32)` declares `coerce[\nat n, nat r, nat v\](x: NaturalNumeral[\n, r, v\]) where { v < 256 } = Byte(v)`; where nothing fixes a type, it is an exact integer, `ZZ`. The coercion's body calls the type's constructor on values computed from the four numbers, as in `coerce[\nat n, nat m, nat r, nat v\](x: RadixPointNumeral[\n, m, r, v\]) = Interval(roundDown(v, r, m), roundUp(v, r, m))`, and the compiler instantiates it for the numeral and folds it (P-176). No numeral value exists when a program runs: a numeral type has no getters, no `asFloat`, no `asString` and no `=` with a number type, and is written only as a coercion's or an operator's parameter type. A numeral has no sign: at a program's type `-3` is the type's own prefix `-` applied to the coercion's result for `3`, and a type without `-` refuses it; any other expression of numerals alone folds first (P-173) and reaches such a type only through its coercion from the folded value's type, such as `QQ` for `1/2`, since coercions do not chain. The design leaves out the binary chapter's numeral coercion and `opr ||` (`Specification-1.0-frozen/advanced-lib/binary.tex:1620-1662`). Why: the Draft gives numerals types of their own so that "library designers [can] decide how numerals should interact with other types of objects by defining coercion operations", and has libraries define the coercions into integers and rationals (`literals.tex:132-148`); the curator finishes that design, in place of the implementations' `IntLiteral` (P-76) and `FloatLiteral` (P-172). Row 1135 holds where the tree departs: walk loading a numeral type written as a static parameter's bound or a `typecase` arm's type. The tree departs too on the compiled path, where a number at a call in a coercion's body runs the declaration that takes it as a `ZZ`, not a more specific one that its conversion reaches (row 1132), and where a numeral at another component's type, whose coercion converts a number that does not fit, is not refused statically (row 1131).
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-bounds-tuple-numeral-type/REPORT.md, "7. Points to report"; from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-numeral-coercion-body/REPORT.md, "Sentences of the specification made false"; commit: 61b50cd34; note: C40, C41. The two sentences on where the tree departs, brought in line with the tree under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"). Rung G of climb batch 36 closed row 1124 (df5948e7b): walk refuses a numeral type at load in a return type, a variable's, a field's or an object's value parameter's type, and row 1135 holds the two forms that still load. Rung N's fork N2 took the getters out of the coercions, and the checker and the literals chapter now convert each number where it stands (F-342, F-345); rows 1131 and 1132 hold the compiled path's two departures, in N's words cut to the rows. His decision, where it applies and what it requires are unchanged.
